    {
      "dom_id": "question-53023",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227606/assets/fae19bb8db103c78bf70bfb65d807f18df344264606282c7a56392a659012f8a.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $VY=WY$ and $VZ=WZ.$",
      "worked_solution": "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment.\n\nHere, the points $Y$ and $Z$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$ Therefore, $Y$ and $Z$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$    \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{YZ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $VX=WX.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}VX & =WX \\\\ 4x-2 & =2x+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
